e  switchboard game and so now this is  powering the switchboards tutorial as an  aside if any of you are interactive  fiction you people used to check out my  tool it's great the slides will be  available later another example so the  first time I showed it I had this  problem where often the make this old  crusty hardware having been shipped  across the country things were going  wrong I didn't know why I couldn't tell  is the hardware malfunctioning what like  why does the internal state of the game  not match the state of the physical  hardware but I already had this concept  of so this engine has like an an output  object that's essentially treating as an  event emitter that it's a vetting these  amounts of like you should turn on this  LED I also already have this iPad build  of the game that works what if I could  just broadcast to multiple outputs and  what if one of those was actually some  JavaScript blue code that connected via  WebSocket to the iPad app and all of a  sudden I had an iPad app that I could  hold and see in real time what the game  thought its internal state was and I  could fix things as need be and because  everything was in place this was maybe  half an hour of work for something like  architecture in this differently  wouldn't have wouldn't have meant oh  this is going to take longer for me to  do like categorically this wouldn't have  been a solution I would have considered  which is fantastic but so I think  everything I'm talking about like this  is this is about weird the games that  use non-traditional interfaces that  doesn't mean that they have to be things  that use embedded electronics I think  sort of anything that is a game that  operates outside of this world of like  here's a game engine and you have  characters moving around I'm like I've  done stuff like this for like web games  and party games I also worked on this  game called When in Rome this is a  tabletop game that you buy on Amazon  like a box of cardboard components  nothing electronic in there but you also  need a smart speaker like an Alexa  device and so Alexa acts a sort of the  game master for this trivia game and as  you fly around the world  you hear interview questions asked to  you by locals in the city as you're  traveling in the game and so this is all  like for those of you who haven't done  any sort of smart speaker app or skill  development this is all web tech they're  sort of a model where when you speak to  your Alexa that gets turned into an  intent which is sort of a data object  representing here is what the user wants  to do that's sent to your web server as  an HTTP request you get to do whatever  you want with it and then send back a  response that has some XML that gets  translated into text being spoken or  audio playing or whatever and if you're  doing the sort of development what can  be a little frustrating is a lot of the  official tools for Amazon or other  platforms like that is the only model  that exists there's no good way to test  your app other than deploying it or  finding a way to like get your local dev  machine on the open Internet so you can  actually talk to your Alexa device and  run through Amazon servers there's no  good way to test it locally or type on  the command line instead of having to  physically talk which is a real problem  when say you're doing this in an office  of a tech incubator where every single  company is building Alexa skills so the  result was something very similar like  we conceptually like the entire game is  a state machine and all of the intents  that you're getting in our edges to make  state machines transitions which means  that like our actual games API as it  were is a single function where you pass  in the intent you were given you pass in  some state that represents like what is  this current user session of the game  and then an output object which again  here is a nice little interface that  knows how to do like mutate able things  to the outside world and then eventually  it returns a new a new state for the  next the next run-through the next time  that user does something and so for the  production build of the game this is  just wired up inside a tiny little node  app that takes the request from Amazon  runs it through this and then that  output objects it's out XML back to  Amazon but we can also have like in  theory here's a is easy to integrate  this with any other voice UI platform  that we've launched up the game on or  more practically here is the command  line version of the game to let me test  without going to Amazon's website but so  we've gotten pretty deep into this of  like alright we're we're writing all  this code to get this Hardware working  why does this actually matter  again if my goal is to make time  management game where you're a  switchboard operator I could have  shipped the iPad version I could have  just written it in whatever I wanted and  orders of magnitude more people would  have played it and I've even heard of  this thing it really matters though like  to me what's the magic of hello operator  is when I show it at events and someone  will come up and like maybe there's  someone who plays games maybe they're  not it is clear they're probably not  someone who would ever like take their  free time and go to a telecommunications  museum but they see this thing and they  immediately go oh that I've always  wondered how that works like how does  that work and then they will sit down  and in this noisy hectic loud show floor  spend like half an hour in a deep state  of flow learning how to do someone's day  job from 90 years ago and that's super  cool like it's it's gamification but the  good kind of gamification like if it was  a little more didactic it wouldn't work  but it works because you're learning  you're learning a real-world skill which  I love designing games based on  real-world skills because you already  have this really nice skill curve like  things that people actually do in the  real world are often already designed as  good games to some extent but also you  know it's an obsolete and useless skill  like it feels like play in a way that it  wouldn't otherwise even though we are  secretly teaching you something which is  awesome and even if you don't care about  that like one layer of abstraction up  like this is kind of obvious but the way  you physically interact with something  shapes your experience of it like even  purely in software using a piece of  software that use every day on your  tablet or on your phone that feels  tangibly different from using the same  thing on your computer even within that  using like a shiny new MacBooks a  butterfly keyboard feels different than  using a nice mechanical keyboard with  nice cherry switches like as an artist  making weird experiences having that as  a dynamic range to play with is really  interesting but even given all that like  maybe maybe you're here at Strange Loop  because you're you're you're a  programmer or your heater program I  think a lot of the strains of audience  is not that but even that like if you  don't care about artistic expression as  its own ideal  doing something with physical hardware  is super interesting and useful and  teaches you a lot about what you do even  if you're just plugging some LEDs and to  an Arduino like what you learn about the  way other forms of nearing work is going  to inform your day to day work like if  you're having to do a literal smoke test  where you turn the hardware on and make  sure it doesn't light up in flames  because you saw it or something wrong  like that might impact how you think  about software testing having to deal  with spending three hours fabricating a  PCB and then having it not work like  that will give you a little more  appreciation for feedback cycles and web  devs and maybe give you a little more  discipline and we're trying to get  things to work the first time but also  above all I'm really just being selfish  like I want more weird things like this  to exist in the world like this is this  is going to talk with a bunch of  technical stuff but it is not a like  deep down technical dive I just want to  show off cool things that I have made so  that you can go be inspired and make  cool things yourself and using using  this idea of you don't have to go and  learn game development or all these  other forms of engineering you can use a  lot of the tools you already use in your  day-to-day life as a programmer and  apply those to making something weird  and cool and exciting and that's all  I've got  Thanks  [Applause]
 

